# Debugging this performance locally

This export includes a small Docker setup for running `performance.ts`
outside the browser, with full breakpoint debugging support - including
stepping into `node_modules` - which the in-browser sandbox can't offer.

## Requirements

- [Docker Desktop](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/) (or any
  Docker Engine with the Compose plugin)
- [VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/), for the debugging step

## Generate `generated.mid`

    docker compose up app

This installs dependencies, runs `performance.ts`, and writes
`generated.mid` back into this folder.

### Writing the output somewhere else

By default the MIDI file is written back into this folder. To send it
somewhere else instead, set `OUTPUT_DIR` to the host folder you want it in
before running the command:

    OUTPUT_DIR=/mnt/c/Users/you/Music docker compose up app

On Windows PowerShell:

    $env:OUTPUT_DIR="C:\Users\you\Music"; docker compose up app

To make this permanent rather than setting it every time, create a `.env`
file next to `docker-compose.yml`:

    OUTPUT_DIR=/mnt/c/Users/you/Music

Docker Compose loads that automatically on every run.

## Step through the code

    docker compose run --rm --service-ports debug

This starts the same run, but pauses on the first line and waits for a
debugger to attach on port 9229. Open this folder in VS Code and press
Run and Debug - an "Attach to Docker" configuration is already set up in
`.vscode/launch.json`. Breakpoints work in `performance.ts` and in any
installed package under `node_modules`, since the code runs as real
TypeScript via `tsx`, not the browser sandbox.
